{"name":"Diamond Dust","hex":"#f8f5e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diamond dust","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ground-level cloud composed of tiny ice crystals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diamond_dust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Diamond dust","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond dust is a ground-level cloud composed of tiny ice crystals. This meteorological phenomenon is also referred to simply as ice crystals and is reported in the METAR code as IC. Diamond dust generally forms under otherwise clear or nearly clear skies, so it is sometimes referred to as clear-sky precipitation. Diamond dust is most commonly observed in Antarctica and the Arctic, but can occur anywhere with a temperature well below freezing. In the polar regions of Earth, diamond dust may persist for several days without interruption."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_dust"},{"title":"Diamond Dust (2018 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond Dust is a 2018 Egyptian crime drama film directed by Marwan Hamed and based on the novel of the same name by Ahmed Mourad. The film stars Asser Yassin, Menna Shalabi, Maged El Kedwany, Mohamed Mamdouh, Eyad Nassar, and Adel Karam. It follows the story of Taha, a pharmacist who lives a normal life with his disabled father until he discovers a series of mysterious murders that lead him to a dark world of crime."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_Dust_(2018_film)"},{"title":"Diamond","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond is a mineral form of the element carbon with its atoms arranged in a crystal structure called diamond cubic. Diamond is a tasteless, odorless, strong, brittle solid, a poor conductor of electricity, colorless in pure form, and insoluble in water. Another solid form of carbon known as graphite is the chemically stable form of carbon at room temperature and pressure, but diamond is metastable and converts to it at a negligible rate under those conditions. Diamond has the highest hardness and thermal conductivity of any natural material, properties that are used in major industrial applications such as cutting and polishing tools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rough_Diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_Diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond"},{"title":"Dust","blocks":[{"text":"Dust is made of fine particles of solid matter. On Earth, it generally consists of particles in the atmosphere that come from various sources such as soil lifted by wind, volcanic eruptions, and pollution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg/330px-Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png/250px-050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png/250px-050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A050207diamond_dust1_Tsukuba_Japan.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png/250px-050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png/250px-050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A050207diamond_dust2_Tsukuba_Japan.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Sun_halo_optical_phenomenon_edit.jpg/500px-Sun_halo_optical_phenomenon_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Sun_halo_optical_phenomenon_edit.jpg/500px-Sun_halo_optical_phenomenon_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Halo display at the South Pole (1980), featuring a parhelion, 22° halo, parhelic circle, upper tangent arc and Parry arc. Diamond dust is visible as point-like reflections of individual crystals close to the camera.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASun_halo_optical_phenomenon_edit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg/500px-Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg/1280px-Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg/500px-Losv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diamond dust in Løkken Verk, Norway.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALosv%C3%A5tt%C3%A5n_L%C3%B8kken_Verk_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/500px-Diamant_gis